WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.060
SQL injection prevention is a practical
engineering responsibility for any Go

2
00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.160
service that talks to a relational
database.

3
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.641
The risk appears when data that a caller
can influence becomes part of the SQL

4
00:00:09.721 --> 00:00:11.687
command text that the database interprets.

5
00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:14.583
That caller-influenced data may come
directly from a request,

6
00:00:14.663 --> 00:00:17.799
or it may arrive after moving through
another service, a file, a queue,

7
00:00:17.879 --> 00:00:18.995
or an internal workflow.

8
00:00:19.075 --> 00:00:21.519
Go helps developers avoid many programming
mistakes.

9
00:00:21.599 --> 00:00:24.681
The language has static types, explicit
errors, clear control flow,

10
00:00:24.761 --> 00:00:26.196
and strong standard libraries.

11
00:00:26.276 --> 00:00:28.774
Those strengths do not automatically
protect SQL text.

12
00:00:28.854 --> 00:00:32.042
The database receives a command at
runtime, interprets its structure,

13
00:00:32.122 --> 00:00:35.895
and applies values according to the API
and driver behavior used by the application.

14
00:00:35.975 --> 00:00:38.154
If the program assembles command text
unsafely,

15
00:00:38.234 --> 00:00:40.997
Go's type system cannot make that command
safe after the fact.

16
00:00:41.077 --> 00:00:42.937
The main prevention habit is separation.

17
00:00:43.017 --> 00:00:45.727
SQL structure should be chosen by trusted
application code.

18
00:00:45.807 --> 00:00:49.421
Runtime values should travel as parameters
or bound arguments through APIs that

19
00:00:49.501 --> 00:00:50.723
preserve that separation.

20
00:00:50.803 --> 00:00:54.151
When reviewers can see that structure and
values are handled differently,

21
00:00:54.231 --> 00:00:57.260
unsafe query construction becomes easier
to spot and easier to fix.

22
00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.209
A Go service should treat HTTP inputs,
JSON bodies, path values,

23
00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:02.096
background jobs, imports, admin tools,

24
00:01:02.176 --> 00:01:05.896
and service-to-service data as untrusted
until the relevant boundary has handled

25
00:01:05.976 --> 00:01:06.560
them safely.

26
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.095
Internal data can still carry risk if it
originally came from a user or outside

27
00:01:10.175 --> 00:01:10.547
system.

28
00:01:10.627 --> 00:01:11.796
The goal is not to fear SQL.

29
00:01:11.876 --> 00:01:15.330
The goal is to make safe query
construction the ordinary path that developers

30
00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:16.260
naturally follow.
